# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# JARVIS — environment configuration
#
# Every provider below is FREE: a key you get with an email address and no
# payment method. Configure one or several — with more than one, JARVIS routes
# each request to whichever suits it, and falls back when one hits its limit.
#
# Copy this file to `.env` and fill in what you have. `.env` is git-ignored.
# Keys are read by the MAIN process only; they never reach the interface.
# You can also paste keys into Settings → AI, where they go into your
# operating system's encrypted credential storage instead.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Groq — the fastest free inference available, and the only provider here that
# also does speech recognition and the neural voice. Start with this one.
# https://console.groq.com/keys
GROQ_API_KEY=

# Google Gemini — the strongest free reasoning, a very large context window,
# and the only dependable free option that can read your screen.
# https://aistudio.google.com/apikey
GEMINI_API_KEY=

# OpenRouter — a wide selection of free models, but roughly 50 requests a day.
# Useful as a fallback rather than a main engine.
# https://openrouter.ai/keys
OPENROUTER_API_KEY=

# Ollama needs no key at all. Install it, run `ollama pull llama3.2`, and
# JARVIS will detect it automatically — no account, no network, no limits.
# https://ollama.com/download

# Optional: a key for a custom OpenAI-compatible endpoint you set in
# Settings → AI (a self-hosted model, a company gateway, another provider).
JARVIS_CUSTOM_API_KEY=

# Optional: override where JARVIS stores settings, memory, routines and logs.
# Defaults to the OS application-data directory.
# JARVIS_DATA_DIR=

# Optional: start in demo mode (simulated activity, no API calls, clearly labelled).
# JARVIS_DEMO=1
